==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|hot-button|hotbutton}}

===Etymology===
Registered as a trademark by American sales trainer Jack Lacy in 1956, first used in the 1940s.<ref>{{cite-book|publisher=United States Patent Office|title=Official Gazette of the United States Patent Office|year=1956|page=TM27|volume=706|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=PTM3wCCwAJIC&pg=PA212-IA47&dq=%22Hot+Button%22&hl=en&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22Hot%20Button%22&f=false}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-au-hot button.ogg|a=AU}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[central]] [[issue]], [[concern]] or [[characteristic]], especially one that [[motivate]]s people to make a [[choice]]; sometimes also one that people seek to delay {{l|en|take sides|taking sides}} on.
#: {{cot|en|hot potato|third rail}}
#: {{collocation|en|was a '''hot button'''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014 |title=Light Up the Night|author=M. L. Buchman |page= |ISBN=1402286988|passage=That issue was mostly solved in the modern forces, but it was still a '''hot button''' for every student of modern military history.}}
# {{lb|en|marketing|originally|US}} The [[principal]] [[desire]] that a [[salesman]] needs to "[[hit]]" in order to make a [[sale]]. {{defdate|from 1940s}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Dental Items of Interest|year=1944|volume=66|publisher=Dental Items of Interest Publishing Company|page=866|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=8x8MAQAAIAAJ&dq=%22Jack+Lacey,+a+top+rating+salesman,+says+that+sales+are+made+by+touching+off+a+'hot+button'+within+the+prospect's+mind+.%22&hl=en&redir_esc=y|passage=Jack Lacey{{sic}}, a top rating salesman, says that sales are made by touching off a “'''hot button'''” within the prospect’s mind.’}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=John D. Yeck|title=How to Get Profitable Ideas|year=1965|publisher=McGraw-Hill|page=197|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/howtogetprofitab0000yeck/page/197/mode/1up|passage=So one way of producing conviction is to “find the '''hot button'''”— something the other person is currently interested in—and show him how your idea can help him get that.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Paul J. Micali|title=Hot Button Salesmanship|year=1975|publisher=Dow Jones-Irwin|page=8|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/hotbuttonsalesma00mica/page/8/mode/2up|isbn=978-0-87094-098-9|passage=Therefore, when a prospect is considering buying something, his main concern is what's in it for him. And he always has a dominant desire. There is something he is trying to do which is important to him. We call this dominant desire his “'''Hot Button'''.” Once you have found the '''Hot Button''' of your prospect, you build your presentation around it.}}
# An [[emotional]] [[trigger]]; something that [[arouse]]s strong emotion or opinions.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|title=From Anger to Intimacy Study Guide|author=Gary Smalley; Ted Cunningham |page= |ISBN=1441225749|passage=Because when you recognize your main '''hot button''', you can take the necessary steps to diffuse anger and reestablish connectedness in a healthy relationship.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|title=The Nanny and the Sheikh|author=Barbara McMahon |page= |ISBN=1460367111|passage=“The sooner he gets rid of those children, the better it will be. He is too busy to be encumbered with orphans,” Delleah said. That struck Melissa's '''hot button'''. “}}

